/-!
# Level-M realisation of the q-expansion function field

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.LevelN.exists_algHom_laurentBaseChange_apply_eq_qExpand`
(`7a3c824d-739d-5148-b55c-0a9f9b438a98`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `462e8492-1441-55ad-8813-de7d0ffe5c16`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Fix $M \ge 1$ and let $A_M =$ [`ModularCurve.LevelN.ring
M`](def/ModularCurve_LevelNFunctionField.html#L28) be the $\mathbb{C}$-subalgebra of functions
$\mathbb{H} \to \mathbb{C}$ generated by $j = E_4^3/\Delta$ together with the Fricke functions $f_v$
for all nonzero $v \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^2$. Let $K$ be a field that is simultaneously a
$\mathbb{C}$-algebra and an $A_M$-algebra, with $\mathbb{C} \to A_M \to K$ a scalar tower and $K$ a
fraction field of $A_M$. Suppose given a group homomorphism $\sigma : \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z}) \to
\mathrm{Aut}_{\mathbb{C}}(K)$ whose kernel is the subgroup generated by $\Gamma(M)$ together with
$-1$ (the join of $\Gamma(M)$ and the integer powers of $-1$), and such that the fixed field of the
image of $\sigma$ is the subfield of $K$ generated over $\mathbb{C}$ by the image of $j$ under $A_M
\to K$. Suppose further given a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra homomorphism $E : K \to \mathbb{C}((q))$
(Laurent series over $\mathbb{C}$) sending the image of $j$ to $\mathrm{qExpand}_M$ applied to
[`ModularCurve.jqModC`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L15), the Laurent series
$q^{-1}\cdot(\text{the integral power series } E_4^3\eta^{-24}\text{ read in }\mathbb{C})$; here
$\mathrm{qExpand}_M$ is the ring endomorphism of $\mathbb{C}((q))$ multiplying all exponents by $M$,
i.e. the substitution $q \mapsto q^M$. Finally let $\Gamma \le \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ contain
$\Gamma(M)$ and the translation matrix $T$. Then there is a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra homomorphism $\Psi$
from [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange ℂ (ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ
Γ)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) — the subfield of $\mathbb{C}((q))$ generated over
$\mathbb{C}$ by the coefficientwise images of the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated over
$\mathbb{Q}$ by all quotients $p_f/p_g$ of integral $q$-expansions of two modular forms $f, g$ of
one and the same weight $k$ for $\Gamma$, with $p_g \neq 0$ — into $K$, such that $E(\Psi(u)) =
\mathrm{qExpand}_M(u)$ for every element $u$ of that subfield, the latter being read as a Laurent
series.

This is the classical statement that the $q$-expansion function field of $X(\Gamma)$, for $\Gamma
\supseteq \Gamma(M)$ containing $T$, is realised inside the field of modular functions of level $M$
compatibly with $q$-expansions, here in a form universal in the chosen expansion map $E$: any
$\mathbb{C}$-algebra map $K \to \mathbb{C}((q))$ matching $j$ with its $q^M$-expansion admits a
compatible $\Psi$. It feeds the comparison of orders of vanishing and the computation of composita
of the function fields attached to $X_1$-type levels.

